[38;2;124;124;124m$u$ and $v$ are adjacent. $N(u) \cap N(v) = \emptyset$ means no vertex is a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mneighbor of both $u$ and $v$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNote: $u \in N(v)$ and $v \in N(u)$, but $u \notin N(u)$ and $v \notin N(v)$ (no[0m[38;2;124;124;124mself-loops). So $u \notin N(u) \cap N(v)$ and $v \notin N(u) \cap N(v)$. So the [0m[38;2;124;124;124mcondition $N(u) \cap N(v) = \emptyset$ is about common neighbors other than $u, [0m[38;2;124;124;124mv$ themselves, which is automatically satisfied for $u, v$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the condition is: for every vertex $u$, there exists a neighbor $v$ such that[0m[38;2;124;124;124m$u$ and $v$ have no common neighbor. In other words, the edge $uv$ is not part [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mof any triangle.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo every vertex $u$ is incident to at least one edge that is not in any [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mtriangle.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWe want to maximize the number of edges.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this. We want a graph where every vertex has at least one [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m"triangle-free edge" (an edge not in any triangle), and we want to maximize [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mtotal edges.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mOne approach: partition the 100 vertices into pairs. Each pair forms a triangle-[0m[38;2;124;124;124mfree edge. Then we can add as many other edges as possible, but we need to [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mensure that for each vertex, at least one triangle-free edge remains.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me think more carefully. If we pair up vertices (50 pairs), each [0m
